CAT = "cmaSafety"; SEG = "SAF"
Q = [
("A patient has ringworm on the scalp. What kind of organism causes it?",
 "A fungus", ["A virus", "A protozoan", "A worm"],
 "Despite the name, ringworm (tinea) is a fungal skin infection treated with antifungals. No worm is involved."),
("A traveler returns with malaria. What type of infectious agent causes it?",
 "A protozoan parasite", ["A gram-positive bacterium", "A yeast", "An RNA virus"],
 "Malaria is caused by Plasmodium, a single-celled protozoan spread by mosquito bites. Antibiotics for bacteria and antivirals do not treat it."),
("Which of these is one of the body's natural barriers to infection?",
 "Stomach acid", ["A fever-reducing drug", "A vaccine dose", "A sterile dressing"],
 "Natural barriers include intact skin, mucous membranes, cilia, tears and stomach acid, which kills many swallowed organisms. Vaccines and dressings are added protections, not natural ones."),
("A patient touches a contaminated exam room doorknob and then rubs her eyes. Which mode of transmission is this?",
 "Indirect contact", ["Direct contact", "Airborne spread", "Vector-borne spread"],
 "Transmission through a contaminated object (fomite) is indirect contact. Direct contact is person to person, such as touching an infected wound."),
("When is an alcohol-based hand rub not enough, so that soap and water must be used?",
 "When hands are visibly soiled", ["Before putting on gloves", "After taking a pulse", "Between two routine patient visits"],
 "Alcohol rub does not remove dirt, blood or body fluids, so visibly soiled hands are washed with soap and water. For routine contacts the rub is preferred."),
("Chemical splashes into a medical assistant's eyes. How long should the eyes be flushed at the eyewash station?",
 "At least 15 minutes", ["About 30 seconds", "About 2 minutes", "Until the stinging stops"],
 "Eyes are flushed for at least 15 minutes with lids held open, then the exposure is reported and checked. Stopping when the stinging eases can leave chemical behind."),
("Used instruments are scrubbed to remove blood and tissue before they are sterilized. What is this step called?",
 "Sanitization", ["Sterilization", "Disinfection", "Antisepsis"],
 "Sanitization is the cleaning step that removes visible debris. Sterilization and disinfection cannot work properly through dried blood or tissue."),
("A tube of blood breaks on the lab counter. What does the medical assistant do first?",
 "Put on gloves and other PPE", ["Wipe it up with paper towels", "Spray bleach on the glass", "Pick up the glass by hand"],
 "PPE comes first. Then broken glass is picked up with tongs or a scoop, the spill is absorbed with the spill kit, and the area is disinfected."),
("When should a sharps container be closed and replaced?",
 "When about three-quarters full", ["Only when completely full", "At the end of every working day", "When the lid starts to stick"],
 "Overfilling a sharps container is a common cause of needlesticks. Containers are sealed and replaced at the fill line, usually about three-quarters full."),
("A new disinfectant's label shows a skull and crossbones pictogram. What hazard does this indicate under the GHS?",
 "Acute toxicity", ["Skin irritation", "Flammability", "Environmental harm"],
 "The skull and crossbones means a chemical can be fatal or toxic on short exposure. An exclamation mark signals irritants, and a flame signals flammable products."),
("Two chemicals in the office carry GHS signal words. Which signal word marks the more severe hazard?",
 "Danger", ["Warning", "Caution", "Notice"],
 "GHS uses only two signal words: Danger for more severe hazards and Warning for less severe ones. Caution is not a GHS signal word."),
("A medical office keeps an ABC fire extinguisher. Which fires can it be used on?",
 "Paper, liquids, electrical", ["Paper, wood and cloth only", "Electrical fires only", "Burning metals only"],
 "Class A is ordinary combustibles, B is flammable liquids and C is energized electrical equipment. Burning metals need a class D extinguisher."),
("In the RACE fire response, what does the first letter stand for?",
 "Rescue", ["Run", "Report", "Respond"],
 "RACE is Rescue anyone in danger, Alarm, Contain the fire by closing doors, and Extinguish or Evacuate. PASS is the method for using the extinguisher."),
("Which drug is routinely stocked on an office crash cart for anaphylaxis and cardiac arrest?",
 "Epinephrine", ["Insulin glargine", "Prednisone tablets", "Amoxicillin"],
 "Epinephrine is the first-line drug for anaphylaxis and is used in cardiac arrest. Crash cart drugs are checked on a schedule for expiration dates."),
("A patient cuts a hand on broken glass and it is bleeding heavily. What is the first action?",
 "Apply firm direct pressure", ["Apply a tourniquet at once", "Rinse it under running water", "Raise the arm and wait"],
 "Firm, direct pressure with a clean dressing controls most bleeding. A tourniquet is for severe limb bleeding that direct pressure cannot stop."),
("A patient slips in the parking lot and the forearm looks bent and swollen. What should the medical assistant do?",
 "Splint it as it lies", ["Straighten the arm gently", "Have her move her fingers hard", "Apply heat to the swelling"],
 "A suspected fracture is immobilized as found to prevent further damage, and circulation below the injury is checked. Straightening it can tear vessels and nerves."),
("A patient with diabetes has fruity-smelling breath, deep rapid breathing and extreme thirst. Which emergency do these signs suggest?",
 "Diabetic ketoacidosis", ["Hypoglycemic insulin shock", "Heat exhaustion", "Hyperventilation"],
 "High blood glucose with ketones causes fruity breath and deep Kussmaul breathing. Insulin shock (low glucose) causes shaking, sweating and confusion instead."),
("A patient is pale, cold and clammy with a weak, rapid pulse after a fall. While waiting for EMS, how should the patient be positioned?",
 "Flat, kept warm", ["Sitting upright", "On the stomach", "Standing to walk it off"],
 "For shock the patient lies flat and is kept warm. Older first aid raised the legs; current guidelines allow it only when there is no suspected spine, hip or leg injury, so check the office's protocol."),
("A landscaper collapses in the heat. His skin is hot and dry and he is confused. What does this suggest?",
 "Heat stroke", ["Heat cramps", "Heat exhaustion", "Mild dehydration"],
 "Hot skin with confusion is heat stroke, a life-threatening emergency: call EMS and cool the patient fast. Heat exhaustion causes heavy sweating, cool skin and weakness."),
("A patient's fingers are white and numb from frostbite. Which first aid is correct?",
 "Rewarm in warm, not hot, water", ["Rub the fingers with snow", "Massage them hard to warm them up", "Hold them near a heater"],
 "Frostbitten tissue is rewarmed gently in warm water and never rubbed. Numb skin burns easily near a direct heat source."),
("An anxious patient is breathing fast and says her lips and fingers are tingling. What should the medical assistant do?",
 "Coach slow, steady breathing", ["Give her oxygen at high flow", "Have her breathe into a bag", "Leave her alone to settle"],
 "Calm reassurance and coached slow breathing correct hyperventilation. Paper-bag rebreathing is no longer advised because it can lower oxygen and hide another cause."),
("After a fall, which sign of head injury needs urgent reporting to the provider?",
 "Unequal pupil size", ["A small bump on the scalp", "Mild redness at the site", "Brief tearful crying"],
 "Unequal pupils, vomiting, confusion or worsening headache suggest bleeding inside the skull. A small scalp bump alone is common."),
("A child's injury has a flap of skin torn loose and hanging. How is this wound classified?",
 "Avulsion", ["Abrasion", "Puncture", "Incision"],
 "An avulsion tears tissue partly or fully away. An abrasion scrapes the surface, a puncture is a narrow deep wound, and an incision is a clean cut."),
("An adult with asthma becomes short of breath and wheezy in the waiting room. Per the provider's protocol, what should the medical assistant do first?",
 "Sit her up, help with inhaler", ["Lay her flat to rest", "Give her a glass of water", "Ask her to hold her breath a while"],
 "An upright position eases breathing, and her own rescue inhaler opens the airways. The provider is alerted, and EMS is called if she does not improve."),
]
